<div class="wrap about-prose">
  <section>
    <h2>How it started</h2>
    <p>The deal was simple: I brought the plan, he brought the legs, and the marathon stopped being an idea. It worked well enough that the next one wasn't a friend.</p>
    <p>The reason never changed. The scale did. Monday is now the same check-in, forty times over, in three languages.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why I stayed</h2>
    <p>I have a finance degree and an MBA. I like that part. What kept me in the sport is doing the arithmetic while I run.</p>
    <p>An example I use a lot: 4×4s for VO2max. Knowing they're the protocol isn't enough. You have to look at whether heart rate actually crossed threshold, how long it stayed up there, and whether the fourth rep was still the work or had turned into survival. That calculation, made on the road, is the same one I make later with an athlete's file.</p>
    <p>Some athletes want to see it. Others just want to know what Tuesday looks like. Both are fine — in both cases I've already done the interpreting, and data that arrives raw leaves as next week's training.</p>
  </section>

</div>

